WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.064
Choosing an IPC mechanism is a security and maintainability decision, not just a performance choice.

2
00:00:06.064 --> 00:00:14.149
Named pipes, local sockets, message queues, platform messaging APIs, RPC-style frameworks, shared memory, and helper processes each make different

3
00:00:14.149 --> 00:00:14.554
tradeoffs.

4
00:00:14.554 --> 00:00:21.831
The right answer depends on trust boundaries, identity support, permission controls, message shape, reliability, observability, platform support, and

5
00:00:21.831 --> 00:00:22.639
failure behavior.

6
00:00:22.639 --> 00:00:24.660
Start with the trust model.

7
00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:34.767
If the receiver needs to know who connected, choose a mechanism that can expose reliable peer identity or can be combined with a strong authorization

8
00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:35.172
layer.

9
00:00:35.172 --> 00:00:42.853
If callers need different permissions, the endpoint should support clear access policy instead of relying on broad global state.

10
00:00:42.853 --> 00:00:47.704
If messages are complex, the mechanism should make framing and validation straightforward.

11
00:00:47.704 --> 00:00:49.725
Performance features need extra discipline.

12
00:00:49.725 --> 00:00:57.002
Shared memory can reduce copies, but it requires explicit bounds, synchronization, mapping size validation, lifetime rules, and cleanup.

13
00:00:57.002 --> 00:01:03.470
Streaming mechanisms are flexible, but they need framing, maximum message sizes, partial-read handling, and backpressure.

14
00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:09.535
Helper processes can improve containment, but they need startup, shutdown, restart, and health-check behavior.

15
00:01:09.535 --> 00:01:10.343
Reviewability matters.

16
00:01:10.343 --> 00:01:19.237
An IPC API that makes identity, permissions, framing, limits, and errors visible is usually safer than one that hides those decisions behind

17
00:01:19.237 --> 00:01:19.641
convenience.

18
00:01:19.641 --> 00:01:30.961
The team should be able to explain why the mechanism was chosen, what it protects, what it does not protect, and how it behaves when the peer is

19
00:01:30.961 --> 00:01:32.982
slow, unauthorized, incompatible, or gone.

20
00:01:32.982 --> 00:01:36.216
Mechanism choice should also consider deployment and operations.

21
00:01:36.216 --> 00:01:40.259
Can the endpoint be configured safely on every supported platform?

22
00:01:40.259 --> 00:01:42.280
Can administrators reason about permissions?

23
00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:47.940
Can support staff distinguish startup failure from permission denial, version mismatch, or resource pressure?

24
00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:53.600
Those questions often decide whether an IPC design remains maintainable after release and upgrades.
